  "summary": "The predefined profiles approach from round 2 is the right instinct but too rigid — instead, model this as 'pipeline recipes' that declare both which phases run AND their data contracts. Looking at the actual code, run_pipeline in phases.py (line 1388) has three distinct sub-pipelines already emerging: (1) the planning loop (plan-inbox/requested-input cycle with plan review retries), (2) spec writing (run_spec_writing), and (3) the impl/test/review loop (_run_impl_test_review_loop). These are natural composable blocks, not individual phases. A board config should select which blocks to include, e.g. {\"pipeline\": [\"plan\", \"spec\", \"impl\"]} for full, or {\"pipeline\": [\"plan\"]} for plan-only. Each block is self-contained with its own retry logic, stage transitions, and data validation — no need to refactor the procedural orchestration. The key insight the previous critique missed is that the phase coupling problem is actually solved by composing at the block level rather than the individual phase level: you never skip spec_writing while keeping implementing, because 'spec' and 'impl' are part of the same block. Implementation-wise, _run_pipeline_impl would just check the board's pipeline config before entering each block, and the filesystem STAGES list in state.py stays unchanged (empty directories are harmless, as round 1 noted). This gives you the flexibility of arbitrary composition without the complexity of a phase graph or the rigidity of 3-4 hardcoded profiles."
